; Test: Sierpiński Triangle of Priorities - Fractal Subdivision
;
; Based on Adam's confirmation:
; "putting (2 5) in between (2 0) and (2 9) should work"
;
; The path encoding is: [arity] <size> bytes...
; So (2 5) = [2] <1> 2 <1> 5
;
; KEY INSIGHT: Tuples with SAME first element but different lengths
; will sort by comparing element-by-element!
;
; (2)     = [1] <1> 2
; (2 5)   = [2] <1> 2 <1> 5
; (2 9)   = [2] <1> 2 <1> 9
;
; Comparison: (2) < (2 5) < (2 9) because:
; - Compare first element: 2 = 2 (tie)
; - (2) has length 1, (2 5) has length 2
; - In tuple comparison: shorter comes first? NO!
; - Actually: (2 5) means "after 2 but before 3"
;
; Let's test empirically!

; ============================================================================
; TEST 1: Basic subdivision - insert between (2) and (3)
; ============================================================================

(level-1-phase (2))
(level-1-phase (2 5))  ; Between (2) and (3)
(level-1-phase (3))

(exec (2)
  (, (level-1-phase (2)))
  (O (+ (result-2))
     (- (level-1-phase (2)))))

(exec (2 5)
  (, (level-1-phase (2 5)))
  (O (+ (result-2-5))
     (- (level-1-phase (2 5)))))

(exec (3)
  (, (level-1-phase (3)))
  (O (+ (result-3))
     (- (level-1-phase (3)))))

; Expected order: (2) → (2 5) → (3)
; Results: result-2, result-2-5, result-3

; ============================================================================
; TEST 2: Finer subdivision - insert between (2) and (2 5)
; ============================================================================

(level-2-phase (2))
(level-2-phase (2 2))   ; Between (2) and (2 5)
(level-2-phase (2 5))
(level-2-phase (2 7))   ; Between (2 5) and (3)
(level-2-phase (3))

(exec (2)
  (, (level-2-phase (2)))
  (O (+ (result-2-again))))

(exec (2 2)
  (, (level-2-phase (2 2)))
  (O (+ (result-2-2))))

(exec (2 5)
  (, (level-2-phase (2 5)))
  (O (+ (result-2-5-again))))

(exec (2 7)
  (, (level-2-phase (2 7)))
  (O (+ (result-2-7))))

(exec (3)
  (, (level-2-phase (3)))
  (O (+ (result-3-again))))

; Expected order: (2) → (2 2) → (2 5) → (2 7) → (3)

; ============================================================================
; TEST 3: Three-level nesting - (2 5 3) between (2 5) and (2 5 5)
; ============================================================================

(level-3-phase (2 5))
(level-3-phase (2 5 3))   ; Between (2 5) and (2 5 5)
(level-3-phase (2 5 5))

(exec (2 5)
  (, (level-3-phase (2 5)))
  (O (+ (result-2-5-base))))

(exec (2 5 3)
  (, (level-3-phase (2 5 3)))
  (O (+ (result-2-5-3))))

(exec (2 5 5)
  (, (level-3-phase (2 5 5)))
  (O (+ (result-2-5-5))))

; Expected order: (2 5) → (2 5 3) → (2 5 5)

; ============================================================================
; VISUALIZATION: Sierpiński Triangle Pattern
; ============================================================================
;
; Level 0:  (2) ──────────────────── (3)
;
; Level 1:  (2) ───── (2 5) ───── (3)
;                      ↑
;                   inserted
;
; Level 2:  (2) ─ (2 2) ─ (2 5) ─ (2 7) ─ (3)
;               ↑               ↑
;           inserted        inserted
;
; Level 3:  (2) (2 1) (2 2) (2 3) (2 5) (2 6) (2 7) (2 8) (3)
;
; Pattern continues fractally!
;
; To insert between (A) and (B):
; 1. If A and B are single elements:
;    Insert (A mid(A,B))
;    Example: between (2) and (3), insert (2 5)
;
; 2. If A is (X) and B is (X Y):
;    Insert (X mid(0,Y))
;    Example: between (2) and (2 5), insert (2 2)
;
; 3. If A is (X Y) and B is (X Z):
;    Insert (X mid(Y,Z))
;    Example: between (2 5) and (2 7), insert (2 6)
;
; 4. If A is (X Y) and B is (X Y Z):
;    Insert (X Y mid(0,Z))
;    Example: between (2 5) and (2 5 5), insert (2 5 3)

; ============================================================================
; ALGORITHM: Fractal Priority Insertion
; ============================================================================
;
; function insert_between(A, B):
;   if len(A) == len(B):
;     # Same depth, insert by midpoint on last element
;     prefix = A[0..-1]
;     mid = (A[-1] + B[-1]) / 2
;     return prefix + (mid,)
;
;   elif len(A) < len(B) and A == B[0:len(A)]:
;     # B extends A, insert by prepending to B's extension
;     mid = B[len(A)] / 2
;     return A + (mid,)
;
;   else:
;     # Incompatible, find common prefix and insert there
;     common = longest_common_prefix(A, B)
;     return insert_between(common, B)
;
; This gives INFINITE subdivision capability!

; ============================================================================
; COMPARISON: Tuple Priorities vs Remy's Cons Lists
; ============================================================================
;
; TUPLE APPROACH (what we use):
; - (2) < (2 5) < (2 7) < (3)
; - Simple, clean syntax
; - Length matters in shortlex
; - BUT: Can subdivide infinitely by appending!
;
; CONS LIST APPROACH (Remy's suggestion):
; - (Cons 2 Nil) < (Cons 2 (Cons 5 Nil)) < (Cons 3 Nil)
; - More verbose
; - Explicit terminator (Nil)
; - Same conceptual subdivision
;
; LINKED LIST WITH BUFFERING:
; - (2 (5 (1 Nil))) vs (2 (5 Nil))
; - Pre-buffering for future insertions
; - More complex to reason about
;
; VERDICT: Tuples work great! Just append elements to subdivide.
